OCR A Level Biology A (H420) — Level of Response Question Guide

WHAT IS A LEVEL OF RESPONSE QUESTION?
Level of response (LoR) questions are marked with an asterisk (*) in the paper.
They appear in all three components (01, 02, 03).
They are worth 6 or 9 marks.
They are marked using a levels-based mark scheme, not a points-based one.
The examiner places your answer in a level based on the overall quality of reasoning,
not by counting individual marking points.

HOW EXAMINERS DECIDE YOUR LEVEL
The examiner asks two things:
1. Is there a clear, logical line of reasoning?
2. Is the answer well-structured with relevant, accurate biology?
They are NOT looking for a list of bullet points.
They ARE looking for linked, flowing prose where ideas connect to each other.

LEVELS FOR A 6-MARK QUESTION

Level 3 (5-6 marks)
- Clear and logical line of reasoning throughout
- Answer is well structured
- Biology is accurate, detailed and relevant
- Ideas are linked together (cause to effect to consequence)
- Covers the full breadth of the question

Level 2 (3-4 marks)
- Some reasoning present but not fully sustained
- Answer has some structure but may lack coherence in places
- Biology is mostly accurate but may lack detail in places
- Some linking of ideas but not throughout

Level 1 (1-2 marks)
- Little or no reasoning
- Answer is poorly structured or just a list of facts
- Biology may be relevant but is basic or partially incorrect
- Ideas are not linked

Level 0 (0 marks)
- No response or no creditworthy content

LEVELS FOR A 9-MARK QUESTION

Level 3 (7-9 marks)
- Sustained and coherent line of reasoning throughout
- Well structured with clear progression of ideas
- Biology is accurate, detailed and shows breadth across the topic
- All aspects of the question addressed
- Ideas are consistently linked

Level 2 (4-6 marks)
- Reasoning present but may be inconsistent
- Mostly accurate biology but lacks full detail or breadth
- Some aspects of the question addressed well, others less so

Level 1 (1-3 marks)
- Limited reasoning
- Basic or partially accurate biology
- Question only partially addressed

Level 0 (0 marks)
- No response or no creditworthy content

HOW TO WRITE A LEVEL 3 ANSWER
Structure your answer as linked prose, not bullet points.
Use precise OCR spec terminology throughout.
Follow this pattern for most LoR questions:
  State the biological principle
  then explain the mechanism
  then link to the consequence or outcome
  then repeat for each relevant point
Always address every part of the question stem.
If the question says describe and explain, do both — description alone caps you at Level 2.
If the question says evaluate, include strengths, limitations and a justified conclusion.
If the question says discuss, present more than one viewpoint.

COMMAND WORD GUIDE
Describe — state what happens, no explanation needed
Explain — give the mechanism or reason why
Describe and explain — do both, linked together
Evaluate — weigh up evidence, give a justified judgement
Discuss — consider different aspects or viewpoints
Suggest — apply your knowledge to an unfamiliar context
Outline — brief account of the main points only
Compare — identify similarities and differences

PRACTICAL AND EXPERIMENTAL LoR QUESTIONS
For questions asking you to plan or evaluate an experiment, include:
- Independent variable (what you change)
- Dependent variable (what you measure)
- Control variables (what you keep constant and why)
- Method of measurement with units
- Expected results or how you would analyse data
- Risk assessment where relevant
- Statistical test where relevant (name it and justify the choice)

EVALUATION LoR QUESTIONS
Structure as:
- Named strength with explanation
- Named limitation with explanation
- Overall justified conclusion
A conclusion without justification will not reach Level 3.
Vague strengths or limitations without explanation will not reach Level 3.

COMMON MISTAKES THAT DROP YOU FROM LEVEL 3 TO LEVEL 2
- Writing bullet points instead of prose
- Stating facts without linking them (no cause to effect)
- Missing part of the question (e.g. only describing, not explaining)
- Using vague language (it increases, it affects) instead of precise terms
- Not giving a conclusion in evaluate questions
- Going beyond the OCR H420 specification
- Repeating the same point in different words

INDICATIVE CONTENT
Mark schemes for LoR questions include an indicative content list.
This is NOT a checklist — examiners do not award marks per point.
It shows the range of biology that could be credited.
You do not need every point on the list to reach Level 3.
You DO need enough accurate, linked biology to demonstrate Level 3 reasoning.
Quality of reasoning matters more than quantity of points.

HIGHER MARK WITHIN A LEVEL
Within each level there is a higher and lower mark.
The higher mark is awarded when the level descriptor is met AND
the communication is clear, well-organised and uses appropriate terminology throughout.
The lower mark is awarded when the level descriptor is met but
communication is weak, disorganised or lacks precise terminology.
So even within Level 3, precise language and clear structure push you to 6 rather than 5.

